msgid "**Read-only profile fields**: for an account that carries the `openid-connect-generic-subject-identity` meta, the `admin_body_class` filter adds `infinito-sso-managed` to the `body` of `profile.php` and `user-edit.php`; the `services.sso.profile.locked` selectors start with that class, so the shared lock renderer of `sys-front-inj-javascript` renders the locked inputs read-only and disables the locked selects, with the translated warning as their hover title. `WORDPRESS_OIDC_PROFILE_LOCKED` lists the keys of `services.sso.profile.locked`, and `INFINITO_OIDC_LOCKABLE_FIELDS` maps each key to its stored field and its `profile.php` input (table below). The `personal_options_update` hook restores each locked input to its stored value or drops it from a `profile.php` save, so WordPress mails no confirmation link to a submitted address, and the `wp_pre_insert_user_data` and `insert_user_meta` filters keep the locked columns and meta on every other `wp_insert_user` write (`user-edit.php`, `/wp/v2/users`, XML-RPC `wp.editProfile`, plugins), except the login's profile sync and WP-CLI. While `email` is locked, an email change of an OIDC account mails no change notice. A field whose key the list does not hold stays editable; the next OIDC login still overwrites it. An unknown key, an empty list or a value that is no JSON list raises on the first guarded write of an OIDC account and names the variable or the key."
